<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Outbound TCP to the SSH server. The only permission the app cannot
         do its job without. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
    <!--
      ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE was declared here to "tell no network apart from
      host unreachable". It was never used: SshService._describeSocketError
      reads the errno straight off the SocketException (ENETUNREACH 101,
      EHOSTUNREACH 113, ECONNREFUSED 111) and asks no ConnectivityManager to
      do it. A permission the code never exercises is one more line for a
      reviewer to query and a user to distrust, so it is gone.
    -->
    <!--
      Downloads land in the shared Downloads collection so they appear in the
      user's file manager. On API 29+ that needs no permission at all — scoped
      storage lets any app contribute to Downloads — so this is capped at 28.
      Asking for broad storage access on modern Android would be both useless
      and alarming.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="28"/>
    <!--
      An open SSH session runs as a foreground service, so switching to
      another app does not make the process a candidate for reclaim (and take
      the socket, the PTY and whatever was running in it along with it).

      FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC is the typed permission that API 34+
      requires to match the service's foregroundServiceType below; dataSync is
      the honest description of a long-running network transfer the user asked
      for. See SessionForegroundService.kt for why the other types do not fit.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC"/>
    <!--
      Requested at runtime the first time a session opens, never at launch. A
      denial is not fatal: on API 33+ the ongoing notification is simply
      hidden, while the foreground service — the part that actually keeps the
      session alive — still runs.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>

    <!--
      Backup is off, on purpose, and the reasoning is worth writing down
      because "allowBackup=false" reads like laziness and here it is the
      opposite.

      Everything this app persists is either a secret or is authenticated by
      one that cannot leave the device:

        * credentials.dart / CredentialStore — SSH passwords, private keys and
          passphrases, held in EncryptedSharedPreferences under an Android
          Keystore key. That key is hardware-bound and non-exportable, so it is
          never in the backup set. Restoring the ciphertext onto a new phone
          would restore something nothing on that phone can decrypt.
        * known_hosts.json — sealed with an HMAC key that lives in the same
          Keystore. Restore the file without the key and KnownHostsService
          fails closed by design: every host reads as unknown and the host list
          shows the "trust store could not be verified" banner. On a brand-new
          device that is indistinguishable, to the user, from the app being
          broken or the phone being attacked.
        * hosts.json — the only genuinely restorable part, and on its own it
          is a list of servers that all prompt for a password next to a trust
          store that just alarmed at them.

      So a partial restore is strictly worse than a clean start, and shipping
      an SSH client's credential store to Google Drive is not a trade this app
      should make on the user's behalf. New device means re-adding hosts and
      re-verifying host keys — which is also what OpenSSH on a new laptop
      means.

      Two attributes are needed rather than one. allowBackup="false" stops
      cloud backup on every API level, but from Android 12 it no longer covers
      device-to-device transfer; @xml/data_extraction_rules is what closes
      that, and it excludes both channels explicitly so neither depends on the
      other's default.

      usesCleartextTraffic="false" is already the platform default at this
      target level and is spelled out because a reviewer will look for it: the
      app makes no HTTP requests at all. It has no bearing on SSH, which is a
      raw TCP socket through dart:io — NetworkSecurityPolicy is consulted by
      HTTP stacks, not by Socket.connect — and the SSH transport is encrypted
      by its own handshake in any case.
    -->
    <application
        android:label="SecureShell Go"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false">
        <!--
          singleTask, not singleTop, and with the app's own task affinity
          rather than none.

          A share arrives with FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK. Under the Flutter
          template's singleTop + taskAffinity="" that started a *second*
          MainActivity in a second task: two Flutter engines, two Dart
          isolates, and so two of everything the app treats as
          process-wide — the live-session registry a share needs to look in,
          and the foreground-service reference count. The user would be
          asked to authenticate again for a host they already had open, and
          be left with two sessions on one server.

          singleTask routes every launch — launcher, share, notification —
          into the one instance through onNewIntent, which is exactly what
          the share intake is written against. Verified on the emulator:
          before this, sharing into a running session produced task t94
          alongside the session's t92.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:resizeableActivity="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
              Puts SecureShell Go in every other app's Share menu for files,
              which is the shortest path there is from "this file" to "on my
              server": share from Files, Photos or a downloads notification,
              pick a saved host and a directory, and the ordinary upload queue
              takes it from there.

              */* rather than a list of types on purpose — the thing being
              uploaded is a file, and an SSH client has no opinion about
              whether it is a photo or a config. The activity is singleTop, so
              a share arriving while a session is open reaches the running
              instance through onNewIntent and can reuse that connection
              instead of authenticating again.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <data android:mimeType="*/*"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <data android:mimeType="*/*"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!--
          stopWithTask="true" because the session lives in the Flutter engine
          attached to MainActivity: swiping the task away destroys the engine,
          so a service that survived it would be an ongoing notification with
          no connection behind it.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".SessionForegroundService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:stopWithTask="true"
            android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync"/>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
